.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt MIXER 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mixer
.Nd the volume, the record source and the input levels, as OSS's mixer
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/soundcard.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/mixer
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The device reads and sets the levels of the audio driver in use, Intel HD
Audio or the Sound Blaster 16, through OSS's ioctls with Linux's numbers:
major 14, minor 0.
It is there only while one of those two drivers is; the PC speaker has no
volume.
The node is mode 0666, so that the console's user sets the volume.
.Pp
A level is OSS's int: the left side's percentage, 0 to 100, in the low byte
and the right's in the next.
Each channel is one level, so a write sets the louder of the two sides
and gives back the level set, and a read gives the level on both.
The channels, by OSS's names, are these; the device mask has those the
driver can set.
.Bl -tag -width SOUND_MIXER_MONITOR
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_VOLUME , SOUND_MIXER_PCM
VOLUME is the hardware master volume, applied to its output amplifiers.
PCM scales only the playback channels opened by the process making the
request, before they are mixed; it starts at 100.
An open inherited across fork belongs to the process that opened it.
A new open takes its process's existing level, or 100 with no existing open.
With no playback open, PCM reads 100 and a write changes nothing.
The console's
.Dv AUDIO_SET_VOLUME
sets the hardware master too.
A master volume of 0 mutes HD Audio's amplifiers.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_MIC , SOUND_MIXER_MONITOR , SOUND_MIXER_LINE
The levels of the microphone jack, the internal microphone and the line
in, as FreeBSD names them, each scaled by the record level for the gain of
the converter's input amplifier on the source's connection.
Each is 100 when the driver starts.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_RECLEV
The record level, which scales every source's level.
It starts at the converter's 0 dB step, 93 on the Lenovo G50-70 and 100
on QEMU's duplex codec.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_IGAIN
The boost of each source's pin, its input amplifier before the converter:
four steps of 12 dB on the G50-70, so that 33 is +12 dB.
It starts at the pin's 0 dB step, and the internal microphone's at
+24 dB, 67 on the G50-70.
.El
.Pp
On HD Audio a percentage is that share of an amplifier's steps, from its
first step at 0 to its last at 100, rounded to the nearest; 0 mutes an
amplifier that can mute.
A source has a channel only when the converter has an input amplifier,
and IGAIN is there only when a source's pin has one.
.Pp
The driver records one source at a time: the internal microphone, else
the microphone jack, else the line in, when it starts.
While a microphone is the source, a plug in the microphone jack moves
recording to the jack, and its removal back to the internal microphone;
a source chosen here stays until the jack next changes.
A change of the source takes effect at once, and one of a level at once
while a stream records and at the next start otherwise.
.Pp
.Xr init 8
sets each channel of the device mask to the level kept from the last
halt, power-off or reboot, if there is one.
The device keeps no level itself, so one set and then lost to a power cut
is not kept.
.Bl -tag -width SOUND_MIXER_READ_STEREODEVS
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_READ_ Ns Ar CHANNEL
The channel's level, the same on both sides.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_WRITE_ Ns Ar CHANNEL
Set the channel to the louder of the two sides, at most 100, and give
back the level set.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_READ_DEVMASK
The channels, a mask of
.Dv SOUND_MASK_ Ns Ar CHANNEL :
.Dv SOUND_MASK_VOLUME
and
.Dv SOUND_MASK_PCM ,
and on a driver that records the sources' channels,
.Dv SOUND_MASK_RECLEV
and
.Dv SOUND_MASK_IGAIN
as the hardware has them.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_READ_RECMASK
The sources the driver can record, by their channels' masks; 0 on the
Sound Blaster 16, which records nothing.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_READ_RECSRC
The source recorded, by its channel's mask, or 0.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_WRITE_RECSRC
Record one of the sources of the mask given: of two or more, one that is
not recorded now, so that a program that adds a source to the mask it
read switches to it.
A mask with no source the driver has changes nothing.
The answer is the source recorded after.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_READ_CAPS
.Dv SOUND_CAP_EXCL_INPUT
when the driver records, since it records one source at a time; 0
otherwise.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_READ_STEREODEVS
0: each channel is one level.
.It Dv SOUND_MIXER_INFO
A
.Vt mixer_info :
the id,
.Dq HDA
or
.Dq SB16 ,
the driver's name, and the count of the writes of levels and of the
record source since boot.
.It Dv OSS_GETVERSION
0x030802, the version Linux gives.
.El
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A read or write of a channel the device mask lacks, such as
.Dv SOUND_MIXER_BASS .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The argument is not a valid address.
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
Another ioctl.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
No driver with a volume is in use.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mixer 1 ,
.Xr console 4 ,
.Xr dsp 4 ,
.Xr input 4 ,
